@link("lyra-wake")
module bindings.wake
//! File descriptors that wake an SDL program's event wait: [watch] has a thread of its own
//! (`wake.c`) wait for them to have data and push an SDL event when one does, so a loop asleep
//! in `SDL_WaitEventTimeout` — an interface with nothing to draw — wakes for a terminal's
//! output as it does for a key. `ui.sdl3` uses it for `ui.app`'s `Readable` subscription.
//!
//! **A watch wakes once, then waits to be armed again**: call [watch] each pass of the loop,
//! after reading what was ready, or a descriptor left readable would wake the loop without
//! end. The event it pushes is one SDL registered for it, which a program ignores — it only
//! ends the wait.
//!
//! Its C half needs no SDL headers (the program links SDL3 itself) and builds into
//! `build/lib/liblyra-wake.a` with `build.sh`.

import std.ffi

/// Wake the loop's SDL wait once when any of `fds` has data to read, its other end closes or
/// it fails — at most 256 — and stop watching any others; none stops watching. The SDL event
/// type the wake comes as, or 0 if the watching thread could not be started.
pub let watch = (fds: []i32) -> u32 => {
  if fds.len() == 0 { return unsafe { lyra_wake_watch(nullptr, 0) } }
  var list = fds
  unsafe { lyra_wake_watch(list.data_mut(), i32(list.len())) }
}

@symbol("lyra_wake_watch")
unsafe extern lyra_wake_watch: (fds: ^mut i32, count: i32) -> u32
